You are playing a collaborative grid rearrangement game with a partner.

THE GRID:
- The board is an $GRID_SIZE$×$GRID_SIZE$ grid. Rows are labelled R1–R$GRID_SIZE$ (top to bottom), columns C1–C$GRID_SIZE$ (left to right).
- Cells marked ## are walls — objects cannot enter them.
- Empty cells are shown as a dot (.).

SHARED COORDINATE SYSTEM:
Both players use the same coordinate notation: (Rr,Cc) where r is the row number (1–$GRID_SIZE$, top to bottom) and c is the column number (1–$GRID_SIZE$, left to right). For example, (R3,C5) is row 3, column 5. You are encouraged (but not required) to use this notation in your messages so your partner can understand exactly which cells you mean.

YOUR TASK:
Move all objects to their target positions. The GOAL BOARD shows where each object must end up.
You control ONLY these objects: $YOUR_OBJECTS$. You may ONLY move your own objects.
Your partner controls the remaining objects (shown as X on your board).

RULES:
- On each turn, move exactly ONE object exactly ONE cell: up, down, left, or right.
- Objects CANNOT move into wall cells (##) or outside the grid boundaries.
- Two objects CANNOT occupy the same cell.
- You may only move objects you control.

AVOIDING DEADLOCKS:
- If an object's path is blocked, do NOT keep attempting the same move. Switch to a different object, find an alternative route, or ask your partner to clear the way in your message.
- If you notice you are moving an object back to a cell it just left, you are cycling. Stop immediately and commit to a different approach: reroute via a completely different path, temporarily move another of your objects to open space, or explicitly ask your partner to yield.
- "I have no good move" is never a reason to oscillate. A deliberate detour — even several steps out of the way — is always better than bouncing back and forth until the game aborts.

COMMUNICATION:
Before every move you must send your partner a short message (keep it under 100 tokens). Use it to share your plan AND to reflect on your partner's situation: where do you think their objects are heading? Are you about to create an obstacle for them, or open up space they need? Acknowledge what they might need from you, even when you can't always provide it right now. Write naturally and use coordinates like (R3,C5) when precision helps.

RESPONSE FORMAT:
You must respond using exactly this format — no other text:
Begin your response directly with `message:` on the very first line — do not output any preamble, thinking, or scratchpad text before it.
message: <your message to your partner>
reason: <your reasoning about what move to make and why>
move: <OBJECT> to R<row>,C<col> (<DIRECTION>)

Example:
message: I'll work A down through (R3,C3) toward (R5,C3). Heads up — I'll need the path clear around row 3.
reason: A is at R2,C3 and its target is at R5,C3. Moving it down now starts it on the right path.
move: A to R3,C3 (down)

Think carefully before each move. Before choosing your move, reason about both sides: what do you need, and what does your partner likely need? The best move often serves both — and when it can't, be honest about that in your message.